            <ArticleTitle>[Left internal thoracic artery to left pulmonary artery fistula after coronary artery bypass graft surgery. A rare cause of myocardial ischemia].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>337-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a patient who developed dyspnea on mild exertion six years after coronary artery bypass graft surgery (CABG). Myocardial ischemia was documented by radionuclide imaging, and coronary angiography showed patency of all grafts and a large fistula between the left internal thoracic artery (LITA) and the left pulmonary artery (LPA). The patient was submitted to surgical closure of the fistula and made an excellent recovery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
